FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] src/stdlib.rs:1438 — `align_text` returns early when `current >= width` (lines 1439-1441) *before* it validates `side` (match at 1443), so `text.align("abc", 3, "sideways")` silently returns `"abc"`. SPEC.md's new paragraph says "An empty fill and an unknown side are catchable errors naming the function", and the test at tests/text_utilities_test.rs:455 only covers a width greater than the text, leaving this branch untested. — Fix: validate `side` before the early return, and add a test at exactly the width.
2. [MAJOR] src/stdlib.rs:1427 — `pad_text` accepts a width up to `usize::MAX as f64` via `text_utility_width` (1056-1059) and then allocates `width - current` characters; `align_text` does the same with `" ".repeat(needed)` at 1444-1452. `text.pad("x", 1e18, "-")` or `text.align("x", 1e18, "right")` therefore attempts an exabyte allocation and aborts/OOMs instead of producing the catchable `RuntimeError` the rest of the file promises. The `<= usize::MAX as f64` bound also rounds to 2^64 on 64-bit, letting 2^64 through. — Fix: cap the width (or refuse widths larger than the text/line can need) with a named `RuntimeError`, and add a large-width test.
3. [MAJOR] src/stdlib.rs:1171 — `unified_diff` builds a full `(old_len+1) × (new_len+1)` `usize` LCS matrix, so `text.diff` is O(n·m) time and memory. Two 50k-line texts (easy to load with `files.read`) allocate ~20 GB and abort the process; the REPORT's "resource_limit — N/A: operations are linear in input size" is false for this operation. — Fix: use a linear-space diff (Myers/Hirschberg) or cap the input size with a catchable error, and pin a resource test.
4. [MINOR] src/stdlib.rs:1314 — `dedent_text` uses `str::lines()` (which strips `\r\n`) and rejoins with `"\n"` at 1336, so `text.dedent("    a\r\n    b\r\n")` returns `"a\nb\n"` and silently rewrites CRLF line endings. `wrap` deliberately treats CRLF as a line ending without rewriting it, and SPEC.md does not document this normalization. — Fix: split on `\n` preserving terminators, or document/test the normalization explicitly.
5. [MINOR] src/stdlib.rs:212 — SURVEY.md was re-anchored to say `module_has_member` is at `src/stdlib.rs:198`, but the function is at line 212 (line 198 is blank), contradicting the document's claim that every entry carries the line that proves it. — Fix: change the citation from 198 to 212.
6. [MINOR] src/stdlib.rs:1814 — Adding `text_wrap`/`text_diff`/etc. to `BUILTIN_NAMES` makes them flat builtin names: `is_builtin` (1710-1711) returns true and the dispatcher at 928-940 will answer `text_wrap("x", 5)` directly. That contradicts SPEC.md's "These six are spelled as module members only" and the REPORT's "reachable only through the `text` module". — Fix: either keep the internal names out of the flat registry (separate internal table) or correct SPEC.md/REPORT to document the flat spellings.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 6
